    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Opening-mode fractures (joints and veins) in layered sedimentary rocks often display regular spacings with the spacing/layer thickness ratio ranging from less than 0.1 to greater than 10, and the most commonly reported values between 0.4 to 1.2 . Existing theories such as the Hobbs model and the stress shadow model give a spacing/layer thickness ratio of 3.0 or higher. Therefore, these theories do not provide a satisfactory explanation of the field data. In this paper, we study the stress state transition between two adjacent opening-mode fractures as a function of fracture spacing/layer thickness ratio using a three-layer model with a fractured central layer. The results show that when the fracture spacing/layer thickness ratio goes from greater to smaller than a critical value, which is approximately 1.0, the normal stress in the direction perpendicular to the fractures changes from tensile to compressive. Opening-mode fractures can not form perpendicular to a compressive stress unless they are driven by an internal pressure that is greater than this compressive stress. Hence, for fractures driven by tectonic strain, the critical fracture spacing/layer thickness defines a lower limit, which also gives the value of fracture spacing/layer thickness ratio at fracture saturation. The critical value of fracture spacing/layer thickness increases with increasing ratio of Young's moduli of the fractured layer to the neighboring layers, and increasing Poisson's ratio of the fractured layer, but it decreases with increasing Poisson's ratio of the neighboring layers. For reasonable variation of the elastic constants of the fractured layer and the neighboring layers, the critical fracture spacing/layer thickness ratio varies between 0.80 and 1.12. This covers the most commonly observed spacing/layer thickness data from the literature for well-developed fractures sets. Further modeling work is need in order to explain all the field data.",
